So what problem docker solves is that it creates a environment independent container which
solves the problem of the general saying that the code worked on my local machine

Docker creates an image which has all the environment dependencies such as Runtime(JDK, python, node etc), our own application, user-space OS dependencies(/bin,/lib,/user)
so when the image creation takes place docker uses Linux kernel from WSL2 (windows subsystem for linux) while when the container runs on linux it uses the linux kernel that's why:
Docker images package user-space OS dependencies, not the kernel. The kernel always comes from the host (or a Linux VM).

Docker uses Linux Kernel for its working

Image : Contains our frozen application, user-space OS dependencies, Runtime
Container : The running instance of image which runs on the host-OS

Difference between VM and container:
We require multiple VM's when different OS's are involved to run different applications so it takes more resources from the Host OS
While containers are lightweight independent of Host OS kernel and runs on the shared Host OS kernel we can run multiple containerized applications on docker->HostOS->Infrastructure

Sure we can also run containers on VM's

Volume/Volumes are the data storages where containers store there data so even if a container is deleted or restarted we don't lose the data but if the volume is deleted we lose the data
also we could use volume to read/write data between different containers (for ex: backend container -> database container)

